    "description": "Ferdowsi was a Persian poet from the Tus region of Khorasan whose Shahnameh reached a major completion in 1010. The epic brought inherited accounts of Iran’s mythical, heroic, and historical past into a literary work that circulated far beyond its author’s homeland. [s1][s3]",
    "tags": ["Ferdowsi", "Shahnameh", "Persian literature", "Khorasan", "Epic poetry"],
    "status": "deceased",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:37:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:37:29Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Ferdowsi and the Shahnameh | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Ferdowsi’s world, the making of the Shahnameh, the legends surrounding his life, and the epic’s wider literary influence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A poet known through an epic",
        "content": "Ferdowsi is associated with Tus, in the northeastern Iranian region of Khorasan, and was born around 940. His familiar name is a literary pen name; sources disagree about his personal and family names. Even basic biographical details therefore require care. Much of what can be reconstructed comes from autobiographical passages in the Shahnameh and from later accounts with different degrees of reliability. [s1]\n\nHe belonged to the dehqans, a landed social group associated with preserving inherited Iranian traditions. Here, dehqan should not be understood simply as the modern Persian word for a peasant. This background helps explain the cultural materials available to him and his sustained interest in the pre-Islamic past. [s1]\n\nA major completion of the Shahnameh is dated to 1010, although the history of its composition included earlier work and subsequent revision. His death date is uncertain. Presenting an exact lifespan without qualification makes the evidence appear firmer than it is. The secure center of his biography is the long literary undertaking itself. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "making",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Inherited stories and a changing political world",
        "content": "The Shahnameh, usually translated as Book of Kings, grew from an existing culture of historical storytelling. A prose compilation completed in 957 assembled pre-Islamic materials in New Persian. Ferdowsi’s project was therefore an act of literary transformation within a continuing tradition, rather than the invention of Iran’s remembered past by a solitary author. [s2]\n\nHis working life crossed a political transition from the Samanid cultural world to the ascendancy of the Ghaznavids. The final version was dedicated to Sultan Mahmud of Ghazni. That dedication situated the poem within the practical world of patronage, where a writer’s hopes and a ruler’s interests did not necessarily coincide. [s2]\n\nLater stories dramatized the encounter between poet and court. One tells of Ferdowsi entering a garden where established court poets tested his abilities before introducing him to Mahmud. Such episodes belong to the poet’s legendary afterlife and should be identified as stories, rather than used as certain eyewitness biography. The distinction allows readers to appreciate their cultural meaning without confusing it with documented events. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What readers encounter in the Shahnameh",
        "content": "The epic moves through mythical, heroic, and historical ages. These are different modes of narrating the past, not interchangeable kinds of historical evidence. Its combination of poetry, folklore, and historiography helps explain why the book can be read both as literature and as a major expression of cultural memory. [s3]\n\nIts transmission also shows that Persian literary culture extended beyond the borders of present-day Iran. The Library of Congress documents a Shahnameh manuscript copied in Iran and another produced in India. The Indian example combines Persian and Mughal Indian features with regional and European artistic elements. The same epic could acquire new visual forms as it moved between readers and workshops. [s3]\n\nThe Library of Congress also traces its influence across Persian-speaking and neighboring literary traditions, including Turkic and South Asian settings. For an international reader, this wider circulation is essential: Ferdowsi belongs to Iranian history, but the history of his poem is also one of exchange across languages and regions. Its continuing significance rests on both its Persian expression and the many communities that have read, copied, illustrated, and reinterpreted it. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
