  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-1-9-5",
    "page_path": "/explore/history/historical-figures/nizam-al-mulk/",
    "name": "Nizam al-Mulk",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/history/historical-figures/",
    "entity_type": "person",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Nizam al-Mulk was an influential eleventh-century vizier serving the Seljuk rulers Alp Arslan and Malik-Shah. His legacy encompasses administration, educational patronage, and the Persian statecraft manual known as the Siyasatnama, whose authorship and transmission require qualification. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Nizam al-Mulk", "Seljuk history", "Statecraft", "Siyasatnama", "Education"],
    "status": "deceased",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:37:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:37:29Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Nizam al-Mulk: Seljuk Vizier and Statecraft | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Nizam al-Mulk’s administrative influence, educational patronage, and the debated textual history of the Siyasatnama."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "career",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Power through the office of vizier",
        "content": "Nizam al-Mulk was the honorific of Abu Ali Hasan, a statesman from the Tus region of Khorasan who lived approximately from 1018 to 1092. The title means order of state. As vizier—a senior minister responsible for government affairs—he served Alp Arslan and then Malik-Shah, rulers of the dynasty commonly spelled Seljuk or Saljuq. His career demonstrates how an official could exercise extensive power without holding the royal title. [s1]\n\nHis family belonged to the dehqans, the landed social group whose members increasingly entered administrative service. He worked in the Ghaznavid world before rising under Seljuk patrons. Detailed stories about these early transitions disagree, however, and medieval biographies often shaped his experiences into exemplary tales of merit, adversity, and eventual recognition. [s1]\n\nHis later reputation as the ideal vizier is itself part of the subject. It should not be treated as an independent measurement of administrative success: admiring anecdotes reveal what later writers valued in a minister, while offering less certainty about individual decisions and their outcomes. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "administration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Administration and educational patronage",
        "content": "One influential scholarly account emphasizes his reliance on established administrative precedents, particularly those associated with the Ghaznavids. Governing involved the relationship between the financial and secretarial administration and the sultan’s court. The distinction helps explain why a powerful minister could direct important machinery of government while still facing limits imposed by royal preferences and court politics. [s2]\n\nNizam al-Mulk also patronized madrasas, institutions of advanced learning especially associated with religious scholarship. The colleges bearing his name, the Nizamiyyas, became a prominent part of his legacy. They belonged to a wider movement: he did not invent the madrasa, and other officials founded and endowed institutions too. [s2]\n\nScholars have interpreted these foundations as supporting Sunni learning, strengthening networks of scholars, and potentially supplying dependable officials. Their purposes should not be reduced to a single proven motive. The distinction between evidence of patronage and interpretation of its political aims is essential, especially when discussing institutions whose functions combined learning, religion, status, and access to power. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "book",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the Siyasatnama critically",
        "content": "The Persian manual associated with Nizam al-Mulk is known as the Siyasatnama, or Book of Statecraft, and as the Siyar al-Muluk, or Ways of Kings. It belongs to the advice literature often called mirrors for princes: works that teach rulers through counsel, historical examples, and moral stories. Its narrative presents trustworthy advice and just government as foundations of durable kingship. [s3]\n\nAccording to the work’s account, Malik-Shah requested guidance on government in 1086. The text developed through an initial composition and additional chapters. Its authorship and surviving form have nevertheless prompted scholarly disagreement. Some defend Nizam al-Mulk’s authorship; others argue that an authentic shorter work was expanded or reworked. The transmitted book cannot simply be assumed to preserve every sentence exactly as the minister wrote it. [s3]\n\nThe manual is therefore most useful when read as an argument about government rather than an administrative audit. Its examples disclose ideals, anxieties, and rhetorical strategies; they do not automatically establish that its recommendations were implemented. Nizam al-Mulk’s enduring significance lies in this conjunction of practical office and political imagination: his career illuminates ministerial power, while the book associated with him preserves a consequential debate about how rulers should govern. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
